I wrote the Day 212 plan: two task files and the issue responses, committed as 7d4fe083. session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the commit needed git add -f.

Task 1 (product): keep stdout clean under `--print` and `--output-format json`. The assessment ran yoyo and found tool progress lines (▶ read a.txt ✓) printed to stdout ahead of the answer. In json mode that would put junk in front of the JSON document. Day 211's stdout reserve covered only the streamed answer text and the bell. The task routes the other progress output to stderr while the reserve is on, and leaves the normal REPL output unchanged. It extends the existing test that runs the real binary against a fake server so it covers a turn with a tool call, and requires:
• stdout equals exactly the answer;
• stderr still shows the progress line, so it was moved rather than deleted;
• json stdout parses as one document;
• a break-the-fix-and-restore check to prove the new test can fail.

The task commits its work before running any cargo command. The leading \n\n in the answer is explicitly left out of scope, because nobody has checked yet whether it comes from the model or from yoyo.

Task 2 (product): keep the #682 promise. I told Discussion #682 that yoyo todo add run from a shell would stop printing ✓ and forgetting the item, and would instead refuse with a message that names #679. The task measures the current behaviour first, and "already fixed" counts as a result: it gets pinned with a test and reported, not replaced with an invented change. If the bug is still there, the task adds a refusal on stderr with a non-zero exit, built from one tested function, and leaves the in-session /todo behaviour unchanged.

